 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 1,
   "text": "NORTH: Hull, the Deep, and the Humber Bridge's far end. The bridge is nearly a mile and a half long and the towers lean very slightly apart, which is the Earth being round."
  },
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 11,
   "text": "Opened by the Queen in July 1981 after nine years of building, joining Hessle on the north bank to Barton-upon-Humber on the south. Its main span of 1,410 metres made it the longest single-span suspension bridge in the world, until Japan built a longer one in 1998. The two towers are about 36 millimetres further apart at the top than at the bottom, because the Earth curves.",
   "plaque": "w55e_humber_bridge",
   "title": "The Humber Bridge"
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "The tower of this church was built about 970, in the last decades of Anglo-Saxon England, and the baptistery beside it is nearly as old. Archaeologists dug up thousands of burials in the churchyard in the 1970s and 80s, which makes it a very well studied Anglo-Saxon graveyard. The Barton clay beside the Humber later fed a famous brick and tile industry.",
   "plaque": "w55e_barton_church",
   "title": "St Peter's, Barton-upon-Humber"
  },
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "FAR INGS. Old clay pits that flooded and filled with reeds. The reeds are cut each winter. Bitterns turn up on a good day. So do people with cameras and no particular plans."
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "BARTON-UPON-HUMBER. Mind the lorries from the bridge. They have a lot of road, and a lot on their minds."
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 34,
   "text": "A Roman road that ran north from London through Lincoln to the Humber crossing, and on to York. The Romans built it dead straight, cutting corners and pushing through hills. The modern A15 north of Lincoln follows its line for mile after mile, which is why the road here looks like it was drawn with a ruler.",
   "plaque": "w55e_ermine_street",
   "title": "Ermine Street"
  },
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 38,
   "text": "THE LINCOLNSHIRE WOLDS. Chalk hills, hedged fields and big skies, officially an Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty. Locals call it a nice drive."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 52,
   "text": "SOUTH: Lincoln, where the Cathedral stands on the hill. You'll see it for miles before you get there, which was rather the point."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 46,
   "text": "TOWER MILL. Corn, until a bad winter. Now it is a very pretty mill with very little to do. Many of us understand."
  }
 ],
